# shell: desktop
# home: fixture
# prefs: factory
# What BUILD means, decided and shown: the project says what it is FOR,
# and the compiler for that target is asked whether its C is good.  What
# it says arrives as messages of the same three things a run's do --
# words, a file, a line -- so the window that shows a run's mistakes
# shows a build's without knowing the difference.
#
# The line a COMPILER means is a line of the file; the line BASIC means
# is a number the program wears.  Landing the caret needs to know which,
# and this proves it does.
wait 25
click 980 30
wait 4
menu-pick Programming
wait 4
menu-pick TikuBasic
wait 150
expect-title Project
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Open Project…
wait 40
expect-title Open
click 400 280
wait 6
click 675 413
wait 60
expect-no-title Open
note the row says what the project is for, which the project itself said
click-window Project
wait 8
click 71 8
wait 8
expect-menu Build for host
menu-pick Build for host
wait 200
note the compiler's complaint arrives as a message, and it is about
note somewhere: the file it named and the line in it
expect-title Messages
note the compiler names the function first and the place second, so the
note first row is about nowhere and the one under it is the complaint
key Down
wait 8
key Down
wait 10
click 71 8
wait 8
expect-menu Go to line 4
menu-pick Go to line 4
wait 120
note following it opens that file at that line -- counted the way a
note compiler counts, which is not how BASIC names its own
expect-title bad.c
key x shift
wait 10
click 71 8
wait 8
menu-pick Save bad.c
wait 90
expect-file-has /tmp/trk_home/bad.c Xreturn nope
expect-file-lacks /tmp/trk_home/bad.c Xint main
